WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
1. Fix and preserve. Stabilise the specimen before further processing. Fixation preserves useful structure while changing living tissue; shrinkage and other artifacts remain possible.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
2. Process and embed. In a routine paraffin workflow, processing removes water and permits infiltration with a supporting medium. Embedding supports the specimen for cutting; orientation determines which relationships the section can show.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
3. Section and mount. A thin cut samples a three-dimensional object in one plane. Transfer the section to glass. One round or oval profile does not reveal the full course of a tube.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
4. Remove wax and rehydrate. Before a conventional aqueous H&E workflow, remove the paraffin from the section and return it to an appropriate hydrated state. This is a conceptual sequence, not a reagent or timing protocol.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
5. Stain for contrast. Hematoxylin-associated nuclear contrast and eosin-associated pink components help reveal architecture. Appearance depends on preparation; pink is not a unique identifier for muscle or collagen.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
6. Finish and examine. Complete the method-appropriate finishing and coverslipping steps, then begin with low-power architecture. Other workflows, including frozen sections, differ from this routine paraffin sequence.
